WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.570
Two code bases,

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.560
same agent,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.590
same task.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.440
Add a rate limiter that blocks a

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.350
user after 100 requests a minute.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.670
The first code base has no tests.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.990
The agent writes the code and stops.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.220
It has no way to know if

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.040
it worked.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.000
Three days later,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.840
production shows the middleware was mounted in

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.930
the wrong order and never ran.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.970
The rate limiter was decoration.

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:28.117
The second codebase has a test suite.

00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:30.037
The agent writes the same code and

00:00:30.037 --> 00:00:31.087
runs the tests.

00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:32.487
Two old tests fail.

00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:33.917
It reads the failures,

00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:34.537
adjusts,

00:00:34.537 --> 00:00:35.647
and runs again.

00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:36.557
Green.

00:00:36.557 --> 00:00:38.197
Then it adds three new tests of

00:00:38.197 --> 00:00:38.897
its own.

00:00:38.897 --> 00:00:39.957
Same agent,

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:41.087
same task,

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:42.407
night and day.

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:44.387
The difference was the tests.

00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:46.599
An agent can't look at a screen

00:00:46.599 --> 00:00:48.109
and feel whether it's right.

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:49.839
What it can do is run your

00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:51.449
tests and read the results.

00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:53.269
Green means keep going.

00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:54.829
Red means try again.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:57.079
No test means flying blind.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:58.969
And the quality of the signal matters.

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:00.669
Expected 429,

00:01:00.819 --> 00:01:03.019
got 200 is something it can act

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:03.639
on.

00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:06.019
A bare assertion error is barely better

00:01:06.019 --> 00:01:06.949
than silence.

00:01:07.815 --> 00:01:09.295
So write the test first,

00:01:09.295 --> 00:01:10.035
then say:

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:11.275
make this pass.

00:01:11.275 --> 00:01:14.195
Picture a duration parser with seven assertions.

00:01:14.195 --> 00:01:16.385
The first attempt forgets seconds.

00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:17.135
Fail.

00:01:17.135 --> 00:01:18.185
It adds them.

00:01:18.255 --> 00:01:19.185
Fail again:

00:01:19.215 --> 00:01:21.255
bad input doesn't raise an error.

00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:22.605
It adds validation.

00:01:22.835 --> 00:01:23.375
Green.

00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:24.955
Each cycle took seconds,

00:01:24.955 --> 00:01:26.915
and nobody had to explain what correct

00:01:26.915 --> 00:01:27.765
means.

00:01:28.095 --> 00:01:30.525
Writing tests becomes programming the agent.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.470
Good agentic tests are fast.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.100
If each run takes minutes,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.660
an agent gets a handful of tries

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.300
an hour.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.540
If it takes seconds,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.510
it gets hundreds.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.840
They're deterministic,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.980
because a flaky failure sends the agent

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.100
chasing a ghost.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.080
They're isolated,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.700
with clear error messages,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.360
and they test behavior,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.450
not implementation.

00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:51.913
You don't need full coverage.

00:01:52.023 --> 00:01:53.703
You need tests where the agent works:

00:01:53.703 --> 00:01:54.843
the hot paths,

00:01:54.843 --> 00:01:56.233
the API contracts,

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:57.963
the seams between components.

00:01:57.963 --> 00:01:59.263
And negative tests,

00:01:59.263 --> 00:02:01.683
so an agent can't simplify your validation

00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:03.233
into accepting garbage.

00:02:04.114 --> 00:02:04.614
Be careful,

00:02:04.614 --> 00:02:05.254
though.

00:02:05.254 --> 00:02:06.894
A bad test suite is worse than

00:02:06.894 --> 00:02:07.614
none.

00:02:07.614 --> 00:02:09.434
Tests that only check the mock.

00:02:09.434 --> 00:02:11.164
Assertions that are too loose.

00:02:11.354 --> 00:02:13.244
Tests that copy the implementation.

00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:15.094
Each one gives a green light that

00:02:15.094 --> 00:02:15.954
means nothing.

00:02:15.954 --> 00:02:17.594
If a subtle bug would slip past

00:02:17.594 --> 00:02:18.374
a test,

00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:19.724
that test is furniture.

00:02:20.516 --> 00:02:21.196
Get it right,

00:02:21.196 --> 00:02:22.956
and the cycle compounds.

00:02:22.956 --> 00:02:25.366
Better tests make agents more autonomous,

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:27.186
which means faster iteration,

00:02:27.256 --> 00:02:29.276
which leaves more time for better tests.

00:02:29.276 --> 00:02:31.516
Your review shifts from does this work,

00:02:31.516 --> 00:02:33.876
to is this the right approach.

00:02:33.876 --> 00:02:35.816
Before you tune prompts or chase new

00:02:35.816 --> 00:02:36.656
models,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.666
go write tests.

00:02:37.836 --> 00:02:38.736
Next up:

00:02:38.736 --> 00:02:40.166
convention over configuration.
